Use one atomic PostgreSQL statement. Do not `SELECT`, increment in Node.js, then `UPDATE`; concurrent requests can read same old value.

```sql
UPDATE counters
SET value = value + 1
WHERE id = $1
RETURNING value;
```

Node.js with `pg`:

```js
app.post('/counter/:id/increment', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = await pool.query(
      `UPDATE counters
       SET value = value + 1
       WHERE id = $1
       RETURNING value`,
      [req.params.id]
    );

    if (result.rowCount === 0) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Counter not found' });
    }

    res.json({ value: result.rows[0].value });
  } catch (error) {
    next(error);
  }
});
```

PostgreSQL locks target row during update. Concurrent calls serialize, each getting distinct incremented value.

If row may not exist, use atomic upsert:

```sql
INSERT INTO counters (id, value)
VALUES ($1, 1)
ON CONFLICT (id)
DO UPDATE SET value = counters.value + 1
RETURNING value;
```

Avoid:

```js
const oldValue = await selectCounter();
await updateCounter(oldValue + 1);
```

If distinct values still repeat, check that response uses `RETURNING value`, reads/writes hit primary DB rather than lagging replica, and no later transaction rollback occurs. For unique monotonic numbers rather than stored counter state, PostgreSQL sequence may fit better:

```sql
SELECT nextval('request_counter_seq');
```

