React compares object props by **reference**, not by their contents. If the parent creates the object during every render, it is a new reference each time:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = { color: "blue" }; // New object on every render

  return <Child options={options} />;
}
```

Even though the object’s contents are unchanged:

```js
{ color: "blue" } !== { color: "blue" }
```

Also, child components normally render whenever their parent renders. To skip rendering when props are unchanged, wrap the child with `React.memo` and keep the object reference stable:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.color}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(() => ({ color: "blue" }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
        Count: {count}
      </button>
      <Child options={options} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Other options include:

- Define a constant object outside the component if it never changes:

  ```jsx
  const OPTIONS = { color: "blue" };
  ```

- Pass primitive values instead:

  ```jsx
  <Child color="blue" />
  ```

- If the object depends on values, memoize with dependencies:

  ```jsx
  const options = useMemo(
    () => ({ color, size }),
    [color, size]
  );
  ```

`React.memo` only skips renders caused by unchanged parent props. The child will still re-render when:

- Its own state changes
- A context it consumes changes
- A memoized prop receives a new reference
- Development `StrictMode` intentionally performs extra renders

Avoid using a custom deep-comparison function unless profiling shows it is necessary; deep comparison can cost more than simply rendering the component.

