JWT `exp` uses Unix time in **seconds**. `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**. Comparing directly mixes units.

Use JWT library verification; it validates signature and expiry:

```js
import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.replace(/^Bearer\s+/i, "");

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Missing token" });
  }

  try {
    req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    next();
  } catch (error) {
    return res.status(401).json({
      error: error.name === "TokenExpiredError"
        ? "Token expired"
        : "Invalid token"
    });
  }
}
```

Manual check, if needed:

```js
const nowSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

if (payload.exp == null || nowSeconds >= payload.exp) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
}
```

Use `>=`: token invalid once current time reaches `exp`.

