```js
const current = await db.query('SELECT value FROM counters WHERE id = $1', [id]);
const next = current.rows[0].value + 1;
await db.query('UPDATE counters SET value = $1 WHERE id = $2', [next, id]);
```

Concurrent requests read same value. Use one atomic statement:

```sql
UPDATE counters
SET value = value + 1
WHERE id = $1
RETURNING value;
```

Node.js with `pg`:

```js
app.post('/counter/:id/increment', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = await pool.query(
      `UPDATE counters
       SET value = value + 1
       WHERE id = $1
       RETURNING value`,
      [req.params.id]
    );

    if (result.rowCount === 0) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Counter not found' });
    }

    res.json({ value: result.rows[0].value });
  } catch (error) {
    next(error);
  }
});
```

PostgreSQL locks row during update. Concurrent updates serialize; each `RETURNING` gets its own incremented value.

If counter may not exist, atomic upsert:

```sql
INSERT INTO counters (id, value)
VALUES ($1, 1)
ON CONFLICT (id)
DO UPDATE SET value = counters.value + 1
RETURNING value;
```

If increment belongs to larger transaction, use same checked-out client:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();

try {
  await client.query('BEGIN');

  const { rows } = await client.query(
    `UPDATE counters
     SET value = value + 1
     WHERE id = $1
     RETURNING value`,
    [id]
  );

  // Other related writes using client.query(...)

  await client.query('COMMIT');
  return rows[0].value;
} catch (error) {
  await client.query('ROLLBACK');
  throw error;
} finally {
  client.release();
}
```

Do not use separate `SELECT` then `UPDATE`. Do not rely on Node mutex; it fails across multiple processes or servers. If only need unique increasing numbers and gaps acceptable, PostgreSQL sequence also fits:

```sql
SELECT nextval('counter_seq');
```

