React compares object props by **reference**, not by their contents. Also, by default, when a parent component re-renders, React re-renders its child components too.

This creates a new object on every parent render:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return <Child options={{ color: "blue" }} />;
}
```

Even though the contents are identical:

```js
{ color: "blue" } !== { color: "blue" }
```

### Prevent unnecessary child renders

Wrap the child in `React.memo` and keep the object reference stable:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div style={{ color: options.color }}>Hello</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(
    () => ({ color: "blue" }),
    []
  );

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
        Count: {count}
      </button>
      <Child options={options} />
    </>
  );
}
```

If the object is constant and does not depend on component state or props, define it outside the component instead:

```jsx
const options = { color: "blue" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child options={options} />;
}
```

Important distinctions:

- `React.memo` prevents re-renders caused by an unchanged parent prop.
- `useMemo` keeps the object reference stable.
- If `Child` updates its **own state**, it will still re-render.
- Changes to context used by `Child` will also re-render it.
- In development, React Strict Mode may intentionally render components more than once to detect side effects.

Also stabilize function props with `useCallback` when using `React.memo`, since inline functions create new references too:

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  // ...
}, []);
```

